{"title":"2025 SSMO Accuracy Round Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_SSMO_Accuracy_Round_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We say that a permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/5/415f82c2e092c5f9407e2c6eba834156d9493581.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_1, a_2, \\dots ,a_{10})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"122\" height=\"20\" /> of the integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> inclusive is <i>peaked</i> if there do not exist three integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/3/7739645df404c3357ad64d0f8888f203339b85c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le i &lt; j &lt; k \\le 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/3/c93fd86c3d242e85ba981ff8797b0e226ad0987a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i &gt; a_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"58\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/8/a38b574bc65ab56ee6c6cf06e6f82192db09f178.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_j&lt; a_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"60\" height=\"17\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/a/22a281826fab0dc40a4eb1bbf99c6f1c19afbe0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{S}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of all peaked permutations. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/8/ef8b17e15273885f41f8ead7ad80f9847d065584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_p = 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"52\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/d/60d6f202b21295aa16dd7eae78f147b018a1ebfb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_q = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"52\" height=\"19\" />, the expected value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/24559fa6a0b0de61b309005d9086967f2f6f05e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|p-q|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" /> over all permutations in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/a/22a281826fab0dc40a4eb1bbf99c6f1c19afbe0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{S}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/5/c85ea1ad780a799bdaf8f327e664f65eb6f3546b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"18\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. What is the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_SSMO_Accuracy_Round_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/7/307f82eee90372caac853a660b3e114620b868bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = (a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_{10})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" /> be an arbitrary permutation in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/a/22a281826fab0dc40a4eb1bbf99c6f1c19afbe0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{S}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be as in the problem statement, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/e/90ef9d8a1e2370962762932152e661ea2ff3007d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le r \\le 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"86\" height=\"16\" /> be the integer such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/7/fc72e2efe9bd83c598c844b635bbeefbfee73717.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_r = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>The key to solving this problem is to formulate a way of uniquely constructing every possible <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. To do this, we first introduce two bits of intuitive terminology; we say that a term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d324291feaa886d6ba5100aa90274b3b1cfa9210.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_x \\ne a_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is <i>lefty</i> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/4/ad4a9cb3a4b564d8131a6140c3e3348bc55351e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> and righty if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/7/e176a3d1d2c1803496ecc0bdd0f79221fd953f07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" />. An example showcases these definitions best: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/a/f7acb06db45003cc259c6748e947dae5a3859b89.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = (\\underbrace{2,4,5,6}_{\\text{Lefty terms}},10,\\underbrace{9,8,7,3,1}_{\\text{Righty terms}}).\\]\" width=\"234\" height=\"42\" /> Assign every term of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> not equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> to be either lefty or righty. We claim that this uniquely determines a peaked permutation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />; in fact, we show that the lefty terms must be in increasing order and the righty terms must be in decreasing order (and if this is the case, it is easy to verify that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is indeed peaked). For the sake of contradiction, suppose that there exist two lefty terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f290847c70b4c7b72554e383d3da5380a9fb7d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{x_1} &gt; a_{x_2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/6410bdfa176299c64df7ea8c8ec86523d79942c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 &lt; x_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" />. However, we then have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/2/4f290847c70b4c7b72554e383d3da5380a9fb7d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{x_1} &gt; a_{x_2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/3/453c10aeab8ec751cc92b3f7d1e354a77d6150c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{x_2} &lt; a_r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"16\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/5/185fbbe92cac742c456eea7878b68ef725debb46.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le x_1 &lt; x_2 &lt; r \\le 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"170\" height=\"17\" />; contradiction. Therefore, the lefty terms are in increasing order. A similar argument proves that the righty terms are in decreasing order.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of indices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/c/63c17c295325f731666c7d74952b563a01e00fcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"9\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/9/699713471f8b35abd9ace00056ea20e60a4d901d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_\\ell$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> lies between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/f/72f6607803caf8c0cfb9b14c99636023bd541a87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_p = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"52\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/f/1cfe5c24f9c6375010214a87c9c6c56a261ac5d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_q = 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"52\" height=\"19\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />; that is, when either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/f/daf0e4301ca91bd0a1607682f363584e292949ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p &lt; \\ell &lt; q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/4/484383d027666139050ed6a5c09d3370eba56982.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p &gt; \\ell &gt; q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"18\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/9/5f9f809daf62865e3a3718c099e64f36edb9ecd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[|p-q|] = \\mathbb{E}[N] + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"168\" height=\"20\" />, so we focus on finding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/3/8231a8c3d991d50b8762a32812485a4ea3844247.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[N]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" />. We do this via casework on whether <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> lie on the same side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or not.\n</p><p><i>Case 1:</i> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> lie on the same side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> (that is, they are both lefty or both righty). Clearly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> does not lie between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Some term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/a/b6ac8c64a2139029433d77e534d6051c49eeba39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\ne 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> can only lie between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/6/606c009bdb53fb9efe1dfda8baf9a513a56caad2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 &lt; d &lt; 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" />; there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Furthermore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must lie on the same of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/8/218c6b3e76211f744168e77b8ad9822ccce27d1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"9\" height=\"21\" />. By the linearity of expected value, the expected value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> in this case is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/0/cb029e830a36dcc90baed169eb7c2223ff5cf99b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\cdot \\tfrac{1}{2}  = 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"68\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p><i>Case 2:</i> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> lie on opposite sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> (that is, one of them is lefty and the other is righty). Clearly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> lies between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Some term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/a/b6ac8c64a2139029433d77e534d6051c49eeba39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\ne 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> lies between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> only when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6570911286ca1d4d732ac5d51bbc620c55a70d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 &lt; d &lt; 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"15\" /> (it is impossible that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/3/b03bc35fc68b7990b763c1ada733bcc803fea606.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9 &lt; d &lt; 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"15\" />); there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> must lie on on the same side of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/8/218c6b3e76211f744168e77b8ad9822ccce27d1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"9\" height=\"21\" />. The expected value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> in this case is thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/d/8cd704cee37d14afdac0c5f4ec1f665c4d9d656a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 4\\cdot \\tfrac{1}{2} = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"99\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>Each of the two cases just outlined occurs with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/8/218c6b3e76211f744168e77b8ad9822ccce27d1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"9\" height=\"21\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/a/82ac83aed96dc88d696c2b184f365e60da2f594c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{E}[N] = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 3 = \\tfrac{5}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"187\" height=\"23\" />. The expected value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/24559fa6a0b0de61b309005d9086967f2f6f05e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|p-q|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/f/f7f63be5d8dfa58d166e70b9a811d3b7b526b805.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{5}{2}+1 = \\tfrac{7}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"79\" height=\"23\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/1/961e4246bca7e7f883270053d2e1496a05196d3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7+2 = \\boxed{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"87\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Sedro\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017135445\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.023 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.033 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 314/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16639/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:33803:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017135445 and revision id 292855. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}